"use strict";(self.webpackChunkultimatelearning=self.webpackChunkultimatelearning||[]).push([[847],{847:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"topic":{"id":"oop","title":"OOP Concepts","overviewSimple":"Think of a toy car. It has wheels, color, speed, and buttons to move. That\u2019s a class! Each toy car you play with is an object. Object-oriented programming (OOP) is about creating things like this, with clear features and actions.","overviewTechnical":["Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in Java is a paradigm based on classes and objects, enabling modular, reusable, and maintainable code.","Core OOP principles include Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction.","Classes define the blueprint of objects, including fields (attributes) and methods (behaviors).","Objects are instances of classes that hold state and provide functionality.","Encapsulation hides internal implementation details and exposes only required functionality via access modifiers and getters/setters.","Inheritance allows one class (child/subclass) to inherit properties and methods from another class (parent/superclass), promoting code reuse.","Polymorphism allows objects to be treated as instances of their parent type, supporting method overriding and dynamic method dispatch.","Abstraction hides complexity by exposing only necessary functionality via abstract classes and interfaces.","Interfaces define contracts that classes can implement, enabling multiple inheritance of type behavior.","Java supports composition over inheritance for flexible and maintainable designs.","Constructors initialize new objects, and overloading constructors allows multiple ways to create objects.","The \'this\' keyword refers to the current object, while \'super\' refers to the parent class.","Static members belong to the class rather than any object, and final members prevent modification."],"story":"Sarah had a magic mold that made toy cats. Each cat had the same features \u2014 like ears, tail, and color \u2014 but behaved differently. The mold was a class, and the toy cats were objects. By changing features, Sarah could make many different cats from the same mold.","realWorldAnalogy":"A cookie cutter (class) creates cookies (objects). Each cookie has the same shape but can have different decorations or flavors (state).","keyPoints":["Classes and objects are the foundation","Encapsulation hides internal details","Inheritance allows code reuse","Polymorphism enables flexibility in object behavior","Abstraction hides complexity","Interfaces define contracts","Composition is often preferred over inheritance"],"exampleSimple":"class Car {\\n    String color;\\n    void drive() {\\n        System.out.println(\\"Driving the car\\");\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nCar myCar = new Car();\\nmyCar.drive();","exampleTechnical":["public class Person {\\n    private String name;\\n    private int age;\\n\\n    public Person(String name, int age) {\\n        this.name = name;\\n        this.age = age;\\n    }\\n\\n    public void speak() {\\n        System.out.println(\\"Hi, my name is \\" + name + \\", age \\" + age);\\n    }\\n\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        Person p = new Person(\\"Alice\\", 30);\\n        p.speak();\\n    }\\n}","interface Vehicle {\\n    void start();\\n}\\n\\nclass Bike implements Vehicle {\\n    public void start() {\\n        System.out.println(\\"Bike started\\");\\n    }\\n\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        Vehicle v = new Bike();\\n        v.start();\\n    }\\n}"],"extraNotes":["OOP promotes maintainability, scalability, and code reuse.","Java enforces strong typing and encapsulation to prevent misuse of internal states.","Method overriding allows child classes to provide specific implementations for inherited methods.","Abstract classes and interfaces provide flexibility in designing systems without exposing unnecessary details."],"bestPracticesSimple":["Keep related things together in a class","Hide internal details using private fields","Use methods to define actions","Create objects to use the class functionality"],"bestPracticesAdvanced":["Follow SOLID principles for maintainable OOP design","Prefer composition over inheritance when possible","Keep classes focused on a single responsibility","Use interfaces to define reusable contracts","Avoid excessive use of static members; prefer instance members for object behavior"]}}')}}]);